{
  "_note": "v3.8: longer visual descriptions alone, without taxonomy: does description length help by itself? Single change from v3 (HM 10.2% top-1 / 30.0% top-5): the visual descriptions are replaced by longer ones (knowledge/disease_descriptions_long_v1.json, mean 20.9 words vs 8.4; written from general knowledge, unverified). Zero-shot has no validation split, so the comparison is on the same test images; about a point either way is within what wording alone can shift.",
  "_result": "Longer is not better on its own: harmonic mean 7.9% top-1 / 30.6% top-5 vs v3's 10.2% / 30.0%. Seen improves slightly (8.9% vs 7.8%) but unseen top-1 halves (7.1% vs 14.6%) and unseen disease accuracy drops (9.2% vs 17.4%); grape_black_rot falls from 59% to 22%. The longer texts share more generic phrases (leaves yellowing, drying, dropping), so descriptions of different diseases become more alike and the distinctive cue that decided an unseen pair is diluted.",
